(   [Problem Statement]: Explain reentrancy and thread-safety in C functions, detailing why internal static state renders functions non-reentrant.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Reentrancy vs Thread-Safety in C:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definition of Reentrancy:) Tj
T*
(   A function is reentrant if it can be interrupted in the middle of execution \(e.g., by a) Tj
T*
(   hardware interrupt, signal, or recursive call\), called again, and safely complete both) Tj
T*
(   executions without state corruption.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. The Hazard of Internal Static Variables:) Tj
T*
(   If a function relies on internal static or global variables to retain context across calls, it) Tj
T*
(   is NON-REENTRANT:) Tj
T*
(   char *non_reentrant_token\(char *str\) {) Tj
T*
(   static char *saved_ptr; /* SHARED STATE! */) Tj
T*
(   /* Interruption here overwrites saved_ptr, corrupting previous caller's state! */) Tj
T*
(   }) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Case Study: strtok\(\) vs strtok_r\(\):) Tj
T*
(   - strtok\(\) stores its internal string pointer in a hidden static buffer. If called concurrently) Tj
T*
(   by two threads or inside a signal handler, the pointer is overwritten, causing silent) Tj
T*
(   corruption or segmentation faults.) Tj
T*
(   - strtok_r\(\) \(POSIX Reentrant alternative\): Requires the caller to supply an external context) Tj
T*
(   pointer \(char **saveptr\), keeping all state strictly on the caller's stack!) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Rules for Designing Reentrant Functions:) Tj
T*
(   - Do NOT use static or global variables for state tracking.) Tj
T*
(   - Do NOT return pointers to internal static buffers.) Tj
T*
(   - Rely solely on caller-allocated memory and local automatic stack variables.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
