(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the stack frame layout during nested and recursive function calls, detailing caller-saved vs callee-saved registers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Stack Frame Layout and Register Preservation Conventions:) Tj
T*
(   1. Standard Stack Frame Structure \(x86-64 Architecture\):) Tj
T*
(   When a function is called, the stack grows downwards towards lower memory addresses:) Tj
T*
(   [High Memory]) Tj
T*
(   | Actual Parameters 7+ \(passed on stack\) |) Tj
T*
(   | Return Address \(pushed by CALL instruction\) |) Tj
T*
(   | Saved Frame Pointer \(RBP of caller\) | <-- Current RBP points here) Tj
T*
(   | Callee-Saved Registers \(RBX, R12-R15\) |) Tj
T*
(   | Local Automatic Variables |) Tj
T*
(   | Temporary Spill Storage |) Tj
T*
(   [Low Memory] <-- Current RSP \(Stack Pointer\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Register Preservation ABI Standards:) Tj
T*
(   - Caller-Saved \(Scratch\) Registers \(RAX, RCX, RDX, RSI, RDI, R8-R11\):) Tj
T*
(   The caller must assume these registers will be overwritten by the callee. If the caller needs) Tj
T*
(   their values after the call, it must push them before calling.) Tj
T*
(   - Callee-Saved \(Non-Volatile\) Registers \(RBX, RBP, R12-R15\):) Tj
T*
(   The callee is obligated to preserve these registers. If the callee modifies them, it must push) Tj
T*
(   their initial values onto stack and restore them before returning!) Tj
T*
ET

